WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Speaker 1: Hello, everybody, producer Pat Forde&nbsp;
here, filling in for David Pakman on another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.800
exciting edition of the David Pakman show. Fear&nbsp;
not, David is going to be back on Monday, but for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.280
today, at least on this Christmas Eve edition of&nbsp;
the show, you are all stuck with me. But that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.080
OK because we're going to talk about a lot of&nbsp;
interesting stuff we're going to talk about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.320
Build back better. We're going to talk about Joe&nbsp;
Manchin. We're going to talk about all Macron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.640
in the latest COVID surge. So let's get&nbsp;
right into the news. As we know this week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.520
Joe Manchin effectively killed the build back&nbsp;
better legislation in its current form. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.800
one point nine trillion dollar social spending&nbsp;
plan is not going to get passed this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.160
and it's not going to get passed in its current&nbsp;
form. Manchin went on Fox News Sunday with Bret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.120
Baer, and he told him, Look, I'm just not going to&nbsp;
support this thing. So as of now, it's dead in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.600
water. Chief among mansion's reasons for opposing&nbsp;
the bill were that he believed it would add to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.320
the national debt. He believed it would further&nbsp;
accelerate the rate of inflation in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.880
States, and he just gave this general sentiment&nbsp;
of Look, we're we're spending so much money as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.560
it is right now. We can't possibly afford to spend&nbsp;
any more. So some of this economic fear mongering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:10.160
stuff that we will often hear from the centrists&nbsp;
and the right wing of this country. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.960
depending on the circumstances. While Goldman&nbsp;
Sachs ran the numbers and they found that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.440
actually not passing build back better&nbsp;
is going to cost the U.S. economy money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.360
because the spending plan would infuse $60&nbsp;
billion into the gross domestic product. A team of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.720
economists at Goldman predicted that the U.S. GDP&nbsp;
would grow three percent in the first quarter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.960
2022 if build back better were passed 3.5 percent&nbsp;
in the second quarter and another three percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.480
in the third quarter of 2022. That's again, if&nbsp;
build back better was signed into law this year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.640
Now, the estimate without the spending plan&nbsp;
in place because they redid the numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.080
is that two percent is going to be the rate of&nbsp;
growth for the first quarter. Three percent is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.080
going to be the rate of growth for the second&nbsp;
quarter and two point seventy five percent in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:01.600
the third quarter of 2022. And when you line up&nbsp;
all of those numbers, you compare the percentages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:08.080
you run the raw numbers. The difference is add up&nbsp;
to $60 billion in economic losses for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.120
three quarters of 2022. And of course, it's&nbsp;
probably going to add up to a lot more than that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.240
because it's only accounting for the first&nbsp;
three quarters of the year. And obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:22.480
the impacts of build back better are going to&nbsp;
extend far beyond 2022. Now, of course, we have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:28.160
include some caveats here. Like, for example, just&nbsp;
because we're not going to get the $1.9 trillion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.880
legislation, there's a good chance that we get&nbsp;
something done right. I mean, maybe it'll be 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.600
billion, maybe it'll be a trillion. Maybe it'll&nbsp;
be one point five trillion dollars. We just don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.280
yet know what the total is going to be, and&nbsp;
we don't know what provisions are going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.160
passed yet. And unfortunately, it seems to be&nbsp;
really up to Manchin and what he wants to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.200
But we'll probably see some boost to the GDP&nbsp;
numbers compared to those more conservative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.400
estimates. Second of all, you can never fully&nbsp;
predict what the U.S. economy is going to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.360
Sometimes we hear estimates from financial&nbsp;
institutions or government agencies about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.640
we expect job numbers to be or what we expect&nbsp;
the stock market to be at months down the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:11.440
years down the line. And it's always just a guess.&nbsp;
I mean, you can make an educated guess, certainly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:16.560
but you never know until it actually happens. You&nbsp;
can't fully predict the U.S. economy and what it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.400
going to do. But these estimates certainly do seem&nbsp;
to jive with the reality of the economy and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.200
we know about demand side stimulus. Because if&nbsp;
you think about it, if you go to these different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:34.320
provisions in the bill, one of the key provisions&nbsp;
is extending the child tax credit of $3000 or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:42.080
$3600, depending on the age of the child per year&nbsp;
per child. It works out so that if people have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.200
this extra money in their pocket, they're going&nbsp;
to be able to spend it on things like going out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.240
dinner or going on vacation or birthday presents&nbsp;
or whatever the case may be. And the same can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:56.720
said about the other provisions, too, right?&nbsp;
Because people can save some money on the cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.160
of elder care or child care or health care costs&nbsp;
or prescription drug costs. And so with that extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.200
money, people can go out and participate in the&nbsp;
economy and it can be economically stimulative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.240
So I understand why this conservative argument&nbsp;
will sometimes when the day, because it just seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.240
so intuitive, right? Like if we just don't spend&nbsp;
the money, that means that we're going to save the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.120
money. But it's actually quite the opposite. And&nbsp;
that's exactly what these Goldman Sachs numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.680
show us. And we, of course, know that Republicans&nbsp;
are going to make this argument. We know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.120
that they love to fear monger about inflation and&nbsp;
spending and the debt and the deficit. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.320
they only do that when a Democrat is in charge.&nbsp;
They don't care so much about these things. When a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.600
Republican is in the White House, we saw that last&nbsp;
year with Donald Trump compared to Joe Biden this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.920
year. I mean, these these things like inflation&nbsp;
and spending, they were all going on under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.160
Trump administration last year. Only now do they&nbsp;
care about these things. But what's upsetting is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.040
that someone who is supposed to be a Democrat, Joe&nbsp;
Manchin, he also buys into these talking points.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.040 --> 00:05:00.240
And ultimately, that's going to harm the people of&nbsp;
West Virginia. And it's going to harm the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.280
as a whole because it's going to cost the U.S.&nbsp;
economy billions of dollars going into next year.

